  "full_response": "Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and User Datagram Protocol (UDP) are two fundamental transport layer protocols in the Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP). They serve different purposes and have distinct characteristics. Here’s a detailed comparison:\n\n### **1. Connection Orientation**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Connection-oriented**: Establishes a connection (via a three-way handshake) before data transfer and ensures a reliable, ordered delivery of data.\n  - Uses sequence numbers and acknowledgments to track data packets.\n  - Example: Web browsing (HTTP/HTTPS), email (SMTP), file transfers (FTP).\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Connectionless**: Sends data without establishing a connection, making it faster but less reliable.\n  - No handshake or connection setup; data is sent immediately.\n  - Example: Video streaming (VoIP, live broadcasts), online gaming, DNS lookups.\n\n### **2. Reliability**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Reliable**: Guarantees delivery of data in the correct order.\n  - Uses acknowledgments (ACKs), retransmissions, and error-checking to ensure data integrity.\n  - If packets are lost, TCP retransmits them.\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Unreliable**: Does not guarantee delivery, ordering, or error recovery.\n  - No retransmission mechanism; lost packets are simply discarded.\n  - Suitable for applications where speed is more critical than reliability.\n\n### **3. Flow Control & Congestion Control**\n- **TCP**:\n  - Implements **flow control** (adjusts data transmission rate based on the receiver’s capacity).\n  - Implements **congestion control** (adjusts transmission rate to prevent network overload).\n  - Uses sliding window mechanisms to optimize data transfer.\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - No flow or congestion control.\n  - Sends data at a constant rate, which can lead to packet loss if the network is congested.\n\n### **4. Speed & Overhead**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Slower** due to connection setup, acknowledgments, and error recovery.\n  - Higher **overhead** (more header data, ~20 bytes per packet).\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Faster** with minimal delay (no connection setup or retransmissions).\n  - Lower **overhead** (smaller header, ~8 bytes per packet).\n\n### **5. Use Cases**\n| **TCP** | **UDP** |\n|",
